; PROVIDER_APPENDED_ZONE_NAME — probably the single most common support ticket
; in this space.
;
; The customer pastes `selector1._domainkey.appended.test` into a provider whose
; UI expects a name *relative* to the zone, so the provider appends the zone
; again. The bug is self-documenting in a zone file: a relative owner name gets
; $ORIGIN appended, which is the exact same mistake.
$ORIGIN appended.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.
@       IN A    198.51.100.10

; Lands at selector1._domainkey.appended.test.appended.test.
; The correct name deliberately does not exist, so a naive lookup gets NXDOMAIN
; and only a second probe at the doubled name explains why.
selector1._domainkey.appended.test  IN TXT ( "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; "
                                             "p=MIIBIjANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAAOCAQ8AFIXTURE1" )

; The same fault applied to an ownership token, which is where it costs the
; most: the token is opaque, so nothing about the value hints that the name is
; wrong, and the correct name is NXDOMAIN with no other clue.
; Lands at _pg-challenge.appended.test.appended.test.
_pg-challenge.appended.test  IN TXT  "propgate-verify=6c1f9a24b7e5d03812af49b6c5d0e7f3"

; And to a CNAME owner name.
; Lands at track.appended.test.appended.test.
track.appended.test  IN CNAME  track.propgate-fixture.test.

; The same provider bug applied to a CNAME *target* rather than an owner name.
link    IN CNAME  track.propgate-fixture.test.propgate-fixture.test.

; And the variant where the trailing dot was stripped, so the local zone gets
; appended instead of the target's.
mail    IN CNAME  bounce.propgate-fixture.test.appended.test.
